"A second objection concerns the test itself. To affirm ones life exactly as it was is one thing for a man who has suffered headaches and loneliness, and another for the victim of a massacre. If amor fati requires us to will the return of every atrocity, it asks for something monstrous. If it asks only that we not let the worst poison our judgment of the whole, it is wise, and much less new than Nietzsche claimed. The Stoics had said as much.": "c40c8285f3bb",
"Apollonian and Dionysian": "e2f9eeda50ad",
"At university he gave up theology for the study of Greek and Latin texts, and lost his faith. He was so able that in 1869, at twenty-four and before he had finished his doctorate, he was made professor of classical philology at Basel in Switzerland. He gave up his Prussian citizenship to take the post and was stateless for the rest of his life. In the war of 1870 he served for a few weeks as a medical orderly, saw the wounded, and came back ill. In Basel he had one great friendship, with the composer Richard Wagner, whom he regarded for some years as the man who would restore to Germany what tragedy had been to Greece.": "01990f067cc7",
"For the next ten years he lived in rented rooms, moving with the seasons between the Swiss Alps in summer and the Italian and French coasts in winter, looking for a climate in which he could work. He walked for hours each day and wrote down his thoughts in notebooks. In 1882 he fell in love with a brilliant young Russian, Lou Salomé, who refused him. In the years after that he wrote the books by which he is known, among them Thus Spoke Zarathustra, Beyond Good and Evil, and On the Genealogy of Morality. Some he paid to have printed. Few sold more than a few hundred copies.": "ba7936492f83",
"Friedrich Nietzsche was born in 1844 in the village of Röcken, where his father was the Lutheran pastor. Both his grandfathers had been pastors too. When he was four his father died of a disease of the brain, after a year of great suffering, and his baby brother died six months later. He grew up in Naumburg in a household of women: his mother, his sister Elisabeth, his grandmother, and two aunts. He was a pious, serious boy who wrote poems and music. A scholarship took him to Schulpforta, the best classical school in Germany.": "0289261010b7",
"He rejected the conclusion. Buddhism, as he understood it, answers suffering by quieting the will, and he called this a weary, late form of wisdom, a passive nihilism fit for a tired civilization. His own answer was to will more strongly, even to will the return of everything. The contrast is real, and it rests partly on a misreading. Nietzsche took nirvana for extinction and the path for a withdrawal from life, as his sources did. The Buddha did not teach hatred of life, and counted craving for non-existence among the causes of suffering along with craving for existence. Where they truly differ is over what the strong response to suffering is. For Nietzsche it is to affirm the wheel and want it to turn again. For the Buddha it is to see clearly what drives the wheel, and to stop (Dharma and Western Philosophy D19).": "ec8b5efd294f",
"His fame began in those years of silence, and his sister took charge of it. Elisabeth had married a leader of the anti-Semitic movement and gone with him to found a pure German colony in Paraguay, which failed. Returning, she gained control of her brothers papers, founded an archive, and published selections from his notebooks under a title of her own, The Will to Power, arranged to suit her politics. She lived to welcome Hitler to the archive. Nietzsche had despised the anti-Semites, his brother-in-law among them, and had called nationalism a sickness. It took scholars half a century after the war to separate what he wrote from what was made of him.": "96a1908de571",
"His first book, The Birth of Tragedy, appeared in 1872. It argued that Greek tragedy arose from the union of two forces, the clear form of Apollo and the intoxication of Dionysus, and that it died when Socratic rationalism taught the Greeks to believe that everything can be explained. Classical scholars were appalled. His students stopped coming. In the following years he broke with Wagner, whose nationalism and hatred of Jews disgusted him, and his health collapsed. He suffered from blinding headaches, vomiting, and failing eyesight. In 1879 he resigned his chair on a small pension.": "04c2dcfb3daa",
"His question was how to live without a world beyond this one to give this one its worth. He proposed a test. Suppose a demon told you that this life, as thou livest it at present, and hast lived it, thou must live it once more, and also innumerable times, with every pain and every joy in the same order. Would you curse the demon, or would you call the news divine? To be able to want ones life again exactly as it has been is what he calls amor fati, love of fate. A person capable of it does not need another world. Nietzsche thought that the moral codes of his age expressed the opposite attitude, a resentment of life on the part of those who suffer from it, and much of his later work is an attempt to trace how that resentment came to be called goodness.": "01e021ef922e",
"If no values are given and each strong person creates his own, nothing seems to stand in the way of cruelty. Nietzsche praises hardness, calls pity a weakness, speaks of higher and lower types of human being, and describes the morality of compassion as a revolt of slaves. Readers who wanted a license for brutality found these passages without difficulty. Defenders reply that he was describing spiritual and not political rank, that the self-overcoming he admired was directed first at oneself, and that he despised the German nationalists who later claimed him. All of this is true, and it does not remove the difficulty. A philosophy that rejects every common standard has given up the means of saying why the brutal reading is wrong.": "13d5e2a5f648",
"In 1888, in a burst of energy, he wrote five short books in a year. On January 3, 1889, he collapsed in a street in Turin. A story told later says that he had thrown his arms around the neck of a cab horse that was being beaten. In the days that followed he sent letters to friends signed Dionysus and The Crucified. A friend came and took him to a clinic. He never recovered his mind. He lived for eleven more years, cared for by his mother and then by his sister, and died in Weimar in 1900. The cause of his illness is still disputed.": "78545934aeb3",
"Nietzsche read what was available on Buddhism in German and came back to it throughout his life. He respected it more than any other religion. In The Antichrist he calls it a hundred times as realistic as Christianity and the only genuinely positive religion to be encountered in history, since it speaks of suffering where Christianity speaks of sin, makes no appeal to a god, and prescribes a way of life in place of a creed. He admired the Buddhas method as a kind of hygiene of the mind: no resentment, moderation in all things, good company, cheerfulness. He thought, too, that he and the Buddha had begun from the same discovery, that there is no fixed self behind our acts and no purpose written into the world.": "9d0bcc7d1e6a",
"Nietzsche wrote in short numbered sections, in parables, and in the voices of characters. He contradicts himself on purpose, tries out a view to see where it leads, and exaggerates in order to wake the reader. He said that he distrusted system builders and that the will to a system is a lack of integrity. The result is that almost any position can be supported with a quotation. Socialists, anarchists, fascists, existentialists, and postmodernists have each had their Nietzsche.": "6fafb2558ca7",
"Nietzsches thought begins from an event. Belief in the Christian God, which had given European life its meaning for two thousand years, was no longer possible for honest and educated people. He did not celebrate this. In the passage where the phrase first appears, a madman runs into the marketplace crying that God is dead and we have killed him, and asks: How shall we console ourselves, the most murderous of all murderers? The bystanders, who are already unbelievers, laugh at him. They have not understood what they have lost. The values they still live by, pity, equality, truthfulness, rested on the faith they have given up, and Nietzsche expected a long age of nihilism, the sense that nothing matters, to follow.": "6cbe745d3b09",
"Two rules help. The first is to prefer what he published to what he left in notebooks. The Will to Power is an editors arrangement of jottings that he chose not to print. The second is to ask of any striking sentence who is speaking and to whom. God is dead is cried by a madman to a crowd of atheists. The remark about the whip and women, which is often quoted against him, is said by an old woman to Zarathustra. Read in context, he turns out to be a subtler writer than his reputation suggests.": "1f207d8c6b74",
